                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "RED is a small free action game that puts its own spin on Little Red Riding Hood. I play as Red as she makes her way through a dangerous forest to visit her grandmother, but instead of simply following the usual fairy tale, I have to fight through monsters that are trying to stop her. It is a simple setup, but I like that the game turns a story I already know into a short side scrolling action adventure.\n\nThe combat is the main thing I am doing here. Red can fight her way through enemies using a mixture of attacks, and the game has a fairly straightforward hack and slash feel to it. I do not have to spend a long time learning complicated systems before I can get into the action. That works well for a smaller game like this because I can concentrate on moving through the forest, dealing with enemies and seeing what is waiting further ahead.\n\nI also like the way the game uses the Little Red Riding Hood setting. The forest gives it a clear identity, and the monsters make the familiar story feel more like a small fantasy adventure. The anime influenced character designs are quite different from the traditional fairy tale imagery, but I think that helps the game feel like its own thing. The presentation is not especially detailed, but it has enough personality to make the world recognisable and keep the short adventure moving.\n\nThe biggest weakness for me is the simplicity. There is not a huge amount of depth to the combat or character progression, so once I understand the basic mechanics, there is not much more for me to learn. The game is also quite focused on its straightforward journey through the forest, which means I am not getting a huge world to explore or a complicated RPG system. I do not necessarily think that is a problem for a free game, but it does limit how high I can rate it when I compare it with games that give me much more to do.\n\nI also think the game feels more like a small project than a fully developed action RPG. The core idea works and I can have fun with it, but some parts of the presentation and overall design are fairly basic. There is enough here to give me a complete little adventure, but I never get the feeling that I am playing something especially ambitious. For me, that makes it better suited to a short session where I want something simple rather than something I am going to spend dozens of hours with.\n\nWhat helps RED is that it does not try to overcomplicate itself. I can start it, understand the premise quickly and get straight into fighting through the forest. I like that kind of straightforward design when I am looking for a smaller game, and the fact that it is free makes the limited scope much easier to accept. It also has Steam achievements, which gives me a few extra reasons to pay attention while I am working through the adventure.\n\nOverall, RED is a decent little free action game that takes a familiar fairy tale and gives it a simple hack and slash treatment. I like the setting, straightforward combat and anime style, and I think the game has enough personality to make the short adventure enjoyable. The lack of deeper systems, limited variety and basic presentation stop it from reaching the higher ratings I give to stronger action games, but I still think it is worth trying if I want something quick and uncomplicated. It is a small game with a clear idea, and for what it is trying to do, I think it works reasonably well.\n\nRating: 6.9/10",
